WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
Many time problems are system problems rather than character flaws.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.900
Overcommitment often comes from unclear priorities, hidden work, optimistic estimates,

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:09.865
and a culture that rewards saying yes.

00:00:09.865 --> 00:00:13.910
Meeting overload can grow because meetings are the default coordination mechanism.

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:18.200
Constant availability can appear helpful while making focused execution nearly impossible.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:22.300
When a system spends time badly, individual willpower is not enough to repair it.

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.600
Multitasking is another common failure mode.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.500
People can switch quickly between tasks,

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:29.300
but demanding work still pays a cost when attention fragments.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:33.390
A person may feel busy all day while never staying with one important problem long enough

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:34.343
to make progress.

00:00:34.343 --> 00:00:36.900
Busyness is visible and often socially rewarded.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:38.000
Value is quieter.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.000
It shows up in decisions made, work completed, risks reduced,

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.440
and relationships strengthened.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:45.380
Perfectionism and procrastination can both distort time.

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:49.170
Perfectionism keeps effort locked on diminishing returns when useful completion would be

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:49.600
enough.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:53.400
Procrastination delays the uncomfortable start until urgency supplies pressure.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.800
All-or-nothing planning adds another trap: if the ideal plan breaks,

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:58.665
the person abandons the whole system.

00:00:58.665 --> 00:01:02.940
A resilient time system expects imperfection and gives people a way to restart without

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:03.380
drama.

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:06.535
Ignoring recovery is one of the most expensive failure modes.

00:01:06.535 --> 00:01:09.500
Sustainable performance requires attention, energy, and judgment,

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:13.100
and those are affected by rest, transitions, boundaries, and reasonable load.

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:15.110
This course is not giving medical advice,

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:17.900
but it is practical to recognize that people are not machines.

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:20.800
A system that consumes recovery may look productive briefly,

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.630
then pay for it through errors, conflict, rework, and lower-quality decisions.
